marvel's avengers is the incredible hulk  of video games  just like bruce banner and his green  alter ego are two people within the same  body  avengers is split between two  personalities one is a single player  story campaign that can be emotional and  thoughtful as you play as each of  earth's mightiest heroes  the other is a multiplayer focused live  game where you punch lots of enemies  without really worrying about why  both sides work in their own way but  they never quite mesh  leaving marvel's avengers a somewhat  confused game but a fun one  despite all its inner turmoil  if we're going to stick with this hulk  metaphor then the single player campaign  is bruce banner  you start the game as kamala khan a huge  fan of the avengers who attends a  celebration called a day in san  francisco that quickly turns tragic the  avengers disband  and after a five year time skip it's up  to the idealistic kamala to convince the  heroes to reassemble  though you play as all of the avengers  over the course of the campaign the game  is at its best as it explores kamala  discovering her own identity as miss  marvel  and helping each of the characters deal  with the trauma and conflicts resulting  from mayday  but this is a superhero game and that  means there are super villains and they  need punching  the core of marvel's avengers is an  action rpg brawler with each character  building combos out of a host of melee  moves  ranged attacks and hero specific special  abilities  combat in marvel's avengers feels akin  to marvel spider-man or the batman  arkham games but the strength of  avengers is while  every character is stamped out of the  same template they all play very  differently from one another  iron man is more of a sniper than a  brawler hulk meanwhile gets buffs from  dealing and receiving damage so he's  best when waiting straight into combat  and smashing as much as possible  and kamala excels at knocking back and  controlling groups of enemies  in the campaign all those characters  allow you to explore different thematic  levels that play to each avengers  strengths but it also causes the story  to feel more disjointed  there's enough difference in the  characters that jumping from kamala to  hulk to iron man over the course of a  few levels  is more disorienting than it is  empowering there's not really an elegant  way for the game to onboard you with  each of the characters so the single  player campaign starts to feel more like  an extended tutorial  to get you ready for the live game  however the story is an engaging one  digging into the same kind of character  conflicts between the avengers that  added depth to the marvel cinematic  universe's take  it largely focuses on the relationship  between the exuberant kamala  and the reluctant banner who's fallen  into a depression and despair in the  years after a day  with banner kamala finds a superhero  mentor who helps her come to grips with  who she's becoming thanks to her new  powers while kamala helps give banner  the strength to step off the sidelines  when he has the ability to change the  world  i wish i could but i can't  but you're dr bruce banner the  relationship between kamala and banner  is what makes the campaign of avengers  work  and the time spent on developing these  characters makes them worth investing in  not everybody in the story gets an equal  amount of attention though  ms marvel banner and iron man are pretty  integral to the story and get a lot of  character growth  but black widow thor and captain america  are largely ignored  in practice the 10 to 14 hour campaign  feels like it shortchanges some of the  heroes to make room to cram in  the hulk side of marvel's avengers the  expansive multiplayer based live game  while many of the missions you play in  the single-player campaign are focused  on a single hero or maybe a pair of them  by about the halfway point they start to  get combined with four rays into the  multiplayer offerings of marvel's  avengers  these are missions of various sizes that  you usually take on with a team of three  other superheroes  those are either controlled by other  players who can join your team through  matchmaking or filled in with ai  controlled versions of the avengers  you've been ranking up throughout the  game  the trouble is couched within the story  campaign multiplayer missions stand out  as being a lot less focused  all the levels even the biggest ones are  pretty homogeneous since they need to  support all different characters equally  that turns them into little more than  big fighting arenas that don't play to  any particular strengths  they also kill the pace of the campaign  cutting back on character moments so you  can run around big chunks of the utah  badlands or the pacific northwest  marvel's avengers has all the trappings  of a live game like destiny 2  or the division with its social spaces  shopkeepers faction vendors and daily  activities  the explanations for all those things  are wedged in the middle of the campaign  and like the multiplayer missions they  feel at odds with what the story is  trying to deliver in its exploration of  its characters  once the campaign wraps up you're left  with just the multiplayer side which  runs on continually throwing more  challenging missions at you and gating  those missions with gear requirements  the live game runs on a loot system in  which you outfit your characters with  equipment that has various stats on it  the overall average of your stats  determines your power level which  dictates how strong you are in a fight  and which missions you're capable of  handling like other live games the  lootchase is supposed to be the engine  that drives your continued engagement  the chance that better more powerful  stuff is the reason to tune in  every day or every week with your  friends  on this front marvel's avengers  flounders a bit just about every piece  of gear has  interesting perks offering advantages  like defense against enemies with freeze  weapons or allowing you to shrink or  poison enemies as you pummel them  but it's only at the very top of the  loop grind as you near the cap of 150  power that you might actually start to  bother looking at the gear you're using  and what it does up until then even  items with good perks will get replaced  in short order since you'll continually  be picking up new gear with higher power  numbers  the good news is the loot chase isn't  really much of a loot grind  you can tear through levels at a pretty  consistent clip which keeps you from  ever feeling like you're gated from  content you want to play  and forced to play stuff you don't in  the end there's a fairly big swath of  activities you can take on  with varying lengths objective types and  difficulty levels  if you want a quick 10 minute experience  you'll have that option if you'd rather  dig in for 30 minutes or an hour or two  hours there are larger levels that can  keep you busy  the tougher they are the more skill they  demand from you in combat  and tapping into that depth is where the  game excels  but like the hulk himself it all feels a  bit chaotic and  unfocused you really don't need to pay  much attention to the loot chase  even though it's ever present in the  game despite a variety of objectives all  the levels are pretty samey  sending you to the same locations to  fight assortments of the same enemies  and when four heroes are wailing on a  big boss or a giant robot  you lose a lot of the nuance that makes  combat good because it's hard to see  through the confusion  [Music]  still marvel's avengers does manage to  scratch that live game itch  just like it scratches the itch for an  emotional superhero story  and when its combat comes together it  can be very fun  there's a lot to learn and master with  each character and their differences  help keep her playing missions fresh  with six characters in the base game  and more coming experimenting with each  one is a solid draw  on top of its disjointed nature marvel's  avengers has been marred with technical  issues and bugs at launch  like missing dialogue checkpoints not  working correctly  and enemies not spawning in during  missions most of these problems are  minor inconveniences  and some seem to have been lessened by a  patch since release  in the last few days though my game has  often suffered crashes and freezes  whenever i've played sometimes i can  play through a whole mission or two and  even get a couple hours out of a  playstation before a crash but other  times the game seizes up repeatedly over  a short period  i've managed to put a lot of hours into  avengers over the last week or so but in  the last few days as i've worked through  the end game  these problems have rendered the game  unplayable at times  time for a prison break on the whole  though i've enjoyed my time with  marvel's avengers  if crystal dynamics can deal with the  technical issues plaguing the game  i'm looking forward to spending time  mastering the combat styles of all the  characters  and exploring the expansions of its  story the live game has set up  as with bruce banner and the hulk it  seems like it'll be worth sticking with  marvel's avengers  to see how it might reconcile the two  halves of its personality in the future  to make something even better  you
 

